well former secretary of state madeleine  albright is warning of a looming  worldwide political threat  during her career in public service  albright met with controversial  political figures including former north  korean dictator kim jong-il  albright argues in her new book fascism  a warning that contemporary leaders are  turning to the same tactics used by  fascists like hitler and mussolini  nearly a century ago  she writes quote we should be awake to  the assault on democratic values that  has gathered strength in many countries  abroad and that  is dividing america at home secretary  albright is joining us now with this uh  book which is so important and comes at  such a pivotal time  in our country and i would say the world  as well you talk about  your family escaping czechoslovakia when  the nazis invaded  only to escape once again once the  communists came to power  talk about any similarities that you see  now percolating  that obviously you went through and the  world went through uh nearly a century  ago  thank you all for having me and and  raising and letting me talk about this  issue um i grew i was born in 1937  and in 1938 an agreement was made called  the munich agreement  which appeased a dictator hitler  and i think that that's a lesson out of  that you have to understand what is  going on  and not give in to evil and then after  the war  when as a result of agreements made  during the war  europe was divided and the communists  quote liberated the red army liberated  czechoslovakia  and so you have to be aware of the kinds  of things that are going on  that these really are kind of one step  at a time and the thing that i'm warning  about  are the steps that are there that we  aren't  paying enough attention to and looking  at what the history  was of mussolini and hitler but also  looking at what's going on now in  countries in hungary where they just had  an election  in turkey uh in the philippines uh in  poland  and in venezuela are just the examples  that are happening now so you're talking  about relentless power grabs essentially  so who is doing the appeasement what can  the world do  well i think what we're doing is kind of  normalizing some of this and thinking  this is just a step  and what is really happening is that  there are uh  leaders that are reacting to something  that is happening  within societies people are not happy  that's for sure some as a result  of technology and they've lost their  jobs some as a result of immigrants  coming into their countries  and these leaders are grabbing power by  identifying themselves with one group  and then  with fear with fear and then also kind  of excluding the individual rights  of other people and being above the law  and undermining  democratic institutions so who's missing  the signals here  is it the american government american  senators the president  is it europe is it the populations where  who are the ones missing the signal i  think frankly everybody because  people do want some answers but i'm  particularly concerned about the  democratic institutions that are  the first article of our constitution is  about congress  and i want them to really understand  what's happening i want to read from  part of your book because i think you  hit that point you write if we think of  fascism as a wound from the past that  had almost healed  putting trump in the white house was  like ripping  off the bandage and picking at the scab  what has trump done well i think part of  the issue is here first of all he's  attacked the free press  which is central to having a democracy  he acts as though he's above the law  he has in fact used kind of rallies and  propaganda and  provided a lot of simplistic answers to  questions  and does not respect the rights of  others yesterday he called  from inside the cabinet room he called  the fbi raid on his personal  attorney an attack on our country well i  find that ridiculous and it's one of  those things where  as i said we can't have a leader that  feels that he is above the law  the law and the rule of law is the most  essential part  of a democratic system can i get your  take on some of the top foreign policy  challenges for this president  well let me just push back briefly on  that which is that republicans would say  look he won an election the american  people elected him  and what his critics will do is the  minute they he does something they don't  like  they say he's a fascist and that that  overstates the case i'm actually not  saying he's a fascist i'm saying  that there are certain elements of the  kinds of behavior that he has  that reminds me of a variety of issues  that have  taken place but i think that this is not  about just this election  this is about what is going on in our  society in terms of respect for  democratic principles  i think trump is the most undemocratic  president i have ever seen  in american history and so that's what  worries me but i do think that we all  need to pay attention  which is why i'm this is a warning  that's what the book is about  when it comes to the national security  of the united states  let's talk about that president trump  has set up what is appears to be is  going to be  uh a global summit or i should say a  summit  with the leader of north korea you've  been there what are the stakes there in  meeting one on one with kim jong-un  well the stakes are very high i'm  actually glad that there is some  desire to have diplomatic talks but i  did go there it took an  awful lot of preparation you don't just  kind of show up  we had been trying to figure out a  variety of things to do  about north korea the former secretary  of defense bill perry did a complete  review all kinds of things  so what worries me is the lack of  preparation  and a lack of understanding of the terms  should we believe kim jong-un said when  he says he's ready to denuclearize  well he says it but his meaning i think  of it is a little bit different than  what we think about  he thinks i think that it's going to be  done in phases  and what we need is to have verification  and complete denuclearization the  president has said  that a response is potentially imminent  to the chemical attacks in syria what  should that response from the u.s  look like and should regime change be on  the table at this point well i think  that what is needed is a strategy we  can't just kind of do one-off bombing  i do think we need i personally believe  that there should be some military  response to what just happened  but it needs to be within an overall  strategy that does i think require  a change uh assad is killing his own  people  and that is just uh it's a war crime and  he may stay it looks like well that's  the issue  and is russia complicit in that yes i  think there's no question  have two presidents in a row been  complicit in that by not taking a  stronger statement well i think that  what happened was president obama  did draw this red line he wanted to have  support from congress because he didn't  want to go outside the law  and then congress did not act i do you  know i think one of the problems about  drawing red lines  and deadlines it ends up being a problem  for yourself so i'm opposed to deadlines  on things  well secretary albright good to have you  here thank you so much  thank you so much and her new book  fascism a warning  is on sale now
 

